  "worked_solution": "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nP\u2228(P\u2227Q)\u2261P.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $P.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P\u2228(Q\u2227P) & \u2261P\u2228(P\u2227Q) \\\\  & \u2261P.\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $P.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Q\u2227P)\u2228P & \u2261P\u2228(Q\u2227P) \\\\  & \u2261P\u2228(P\u2227Q) \\\\  & \u2261P.\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $P.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
